 me a lot  of hope in a place like this  all you got is hope that's what you hold  on to hope that you're going to get out  one day  if flaherty's 40-year sentence for  robbery seems excessive  consider bobby bostick he was 16  in 1995 when he and a friend committed  an armed robbery and a carjacking at  christmas time  boss 638 firearm was real and one of his  victims was grazed by a bullet  did you intend to hurt him to kill him  no if i had tend to her to kill him i'd  have kept shooting  but i didn't tend to hurt him off shoot  he's just i was in my young mind  it's no excuse when i look back it's no  excuse for what i did  but bostick a juvenile never expected  the judge to rule so  harshly i didn't think i'd get 30. and  how many years did you get 241 years  did you think he was irredeemable  yes at the time judge evelyn baker now  retired  thought the sentence was appropriate for  bostick i didn't expect him to survive  prison  i figured with this behavior she was  going to wind up dead  but instead when he had money bostick  spent it on college classes  and the teen who never read a book wrote  15  behind bars and he has held on to hope  even when he watches those with murder  convictions  go home ahead of him got something to  give to the world  so i got to act on that hope i got to  just keep  going even when this appeal get denied i  got to keep going because one day  something happened and remarkably the  person  who most wants to see that happen 241  years is insanity  when i think back on it and i'll say  right now it's insanity  he was a kid he was a little boy but  you're the one who sentenced him judge  yes as he evolved i have evolved  i mean is it fair to say that the case  of bobby bostic  has haunted you that would be a very  true statement  i don't want to go to my grave with  bobby incarcerated  i think bobby has so much that he can  give to this world  do you think that someday bobby bosick  will be walking out the same door  100 i think i think next year  well there is hope for bobby bostic  there is a new bill sitting on missouri  governor parsons desk  waiting for a signature which will give  bosek and others who were sentences  sentenced as juveniles the ability to  petition the parole board  for a hearing but that is no guarantee  that basic will be released  he is a lot closer i do want to point  out under this very complicated formula  if the governor does not sign that bill  basic is eligible  for parole wait for it when he's 112  years old i know aaron you hear that  story and you you have to wonder why he  is not out today  yes that somebody looking at this story  hopefully will step in this makes no  sense to me well this is why the judge  is speaking out  um she actually even helped him petition  the supreme court in 2017.  is there more she can or should do no  it's very interesting i think people are  surprised to hear that judges can't do a  do-over yeah exactly  the judge could say i made a mistake i'm  changing this ruling but she is coming  out she's even visited him in prison  and she feels very strongly these guys  are forgotten  which is why i just wonder how she came  up with the 241 years in the first  place well there were 17 counts and then  he was  you know convicted of all and then she  made them consecutive  and that's why it's but the judge has  discretion in those cases  exactly you can believe in justice and  not want to throw away a child a 16 year  old  but he didn't look like he had remorse  that's what she thought at the time  you heard her say she thought he was  gonna die in prison exactly but as she  says as he has evolved  i have evolved i'm glad she said that  but bobby needs to be out today  thank you aaron
 

